✅ What is a Sticky Bit in Linux?

The Sticky Bit is a special Linux permission mainly used on shared directories. It allows users to create files in the directory, but only the file owner, directory owner, or root can delete or rename those files.

For /tmp --> ls -ld /tmp
 
Example:
 
drwxrwxrwt root root /tmp
 
The t at the end indicates that the Sticky Bit is enabled.
 
To set Sticky Bit --> chmod +t directory
To remove --> chmod -t directory
