✅ What is the difference between a process and a thread?

A process is an independent running program with its own memory space and resources. A thread is a lightweight execution unit within a process that shares the process's memory and resources. Threads are generally faster to create and communicate with than separate processes.
The process provides the memory/resources, while its threads perform the actual execution.

To check processes --> ps -ef
To check Thread --> ps -eLf (or) --> 'top' command, Then press H to display threads.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ What are the different types of processes?

Linux processes can be classified as foreground, background, daemon, parent, child, orphan, and zombie processes. Foreground and background describe how a process runs, while parent/child describe the process relationship. An orphan has lost its parent, whereas a zombie has already terminated but its exit status hasn't been collected.

1. Foreground Process --> The command runs interactively and occupies your terminal.
2. Background Process -->The process runs in the background and you can continue using the terminal.
3. Daemon Process --> Daemons normally run in the background and provide services.
4. Zombie Process --> A zombie is a terminated process whose exit status has not yet been collected by its parent.
5. Orphan Process --> An orphan is a child process whose parent has terminated. Linux re-parents it to an appropriate system process, commonly systemd (PID 1).
