✅ How would you continuously monitor logs in Linux?

 Use tail -f to continuously monitor a log file. For systemd services, use journalctl -f or journalctl -u <service> -f. This helps monitor real-time events while troubleshooting production issues.

The most common cmd --> tail -f /var/log/messages
It continuously displays new log entries as they are added to the file

Monitor last 100 lines and continue watching --> tail -n 100 -f /var/log/messages

Follow logs using 'less' --> less +F /var/log/messages
Press Ctrl+C to stop following the file.

On systemd-based Linux --> journalctl -u sshd -f

Filter specific errors --> tail -f /var/log/messages | grep -i error